1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:23,686
Aurelio Cruel entered the sixth race himself. The announcement came
from Isla Gloria at breakfast. His Grace would personally defend the
honor of the Grand Prix in the Bridge of Kings, a crossing of the
floating road between the island's eastern harbor and the old naval
fort. Tank looked at the photograph of the course. Nigga, that's water
with opinions.

2
00:00:23,936 --> 00:00:48,925
The road had been assembled from broad linked platforms, each wide
enough for two cars, stretching across a sheltered bay. Boats held the
outer sections steady. At the far end, a steep ramp climbed onto the
fort's quay. The drivers would cross twice, circle the island's
coastal road and finish at the palace gate. Nero studied the picture.
It used to be a ceremonial crossing.

3
00:00:49,175 --> 00:01:10,159
Slow. Flags. Music. Nigga made everything a threat, I said. Yes.
Celadon stood beside him, arms folded. The platforms can carry the
cars. The danger is what he asks the drivers to do on them. I looked
toward the harbor, where the Sovereign Sun now flew its old white bird
above the bridge.

4
00:01:10,409 --> 00:01:29,857
Celadon had reclaimed the ship in every way that mattered to the
people who worked aboard it. The duke still claimed to own it. He also
claimed I had ruined the winter garden, which was technically true in
enough places to make arguing tedious. Tank stood at the dock counting
the cars going aboard.

5
00:01:30,107 --> 00:01:52,325
"Nigga, why we bringing a boat to a car race?" "Because the road in
the water," I said. "And why the road in the water?" "Because that
nigga owns an island." "So when you own it, we fixing that?" Shawna
looked over her shoulder. "Nigga, he'll put another road higher up and
race both." "See?" Tank said to Bruno. "That nigga ain't a solution.

6
00:01:52,575 --> 00:02:16,191
That nigga's more geography." Bruno laughed and ducked beneath the
gangway frame. We took the flagship to the race. Nigga, if somebody
steals a man's boat and that man gets it back, you ride with the man.
The whole crew came aboard in the morning. Lark and Mateo loaded the
Lamborghini. Tank brought the replacement black coupe. Porsche brought
her Ferrari.

7
00:02:16,441 --> 00:02:41,401
Cassius drove the White Heron onto the lower deck with Nero beside
him. Inez arrived in red and kissed Cassius before handing him his
gloves, a gesture so ordinary now that both their fathers looked away
to give it room. Shawna carried her music in a blue case. Rehearsal
after? I asked. Victory after. Then rehearsal. Nigga, order matters.
It does.

8
00:02:41,651 --> 00:03:03,899
We sailed into Isla Gloria's harbor with the ship's bell ringing.
People crowded the quay. They saw Celadon on the bridge, saw Nero
standing beside him and began cheering before they even found me on
the deck. I liked that. Then they found me. Nigga, I liked that too.
The duke waited at the starting area in a green racing suit.

9
00:03:04,149 --> 00:03:26,173
His car was black and gold, long, low and ugly with money. He wore no
mask of courtesy now. The white streaks in his hair stood out sharply
against the dark suit, and his eyes followed me with a kind of hunger
that had forgotten how to look charming. You brought stolen property
to my island, he said. Celadon stepped forward.

10
00:03:26,423 --> 00:03:49,193
I brought my ship home. Aurelio looked at him. For a moment I saw the
old habit in his face: speak sharply, summon a guard, return the man
to a room. Then he saw Nero. Cassius. Odette's officers on the quay.
The cameras. The crowd pressing close enough to hear every word. He
swallowed the command. Nigga, it looked painful.

11
00:03:49,443 --> 00:04:12,373
The race, he said, will proceed. I stood beside my Lamborghini. Good.
You been talking for three weeks. His jaw tightened. You think these
people love you. I looked around. A woman held up a loaf of bread from
the bakery. Julian stood beside her, waving both arms. Alina stood
with Mercedes and Porsche on the viewing platform.

12
00:04:12,623 --> 00:04:29,486
Rocco wore a blue shirt with my face on it and looked delighted to
have found a profitable form of courage. Nigga, I said, ask them. He
didn't. We lined up at the floating road. Six cars entered. Inez and
Cassius had chosen to race.

13
00:04:29,736 --> 00:04:52,034
Adrian joined too, along with a veteran driver named Sabine Cross,
forty-one, who had told a reporter she considered Aiden impossible but
wanted to confirm it at close range. I respected scientific curiosity.
The first horn sounded. The platforms moved gently beneath our wheels.
Water shone on both sides.

14
00:04:52,284 --> 00:05:11,757
The road ahead rose and fell in long shallow motions, never quite
still, making distance a thing you had to feel as much as see. The
second horn sounded. The duke looked across at me. I smiled. The third
horn sent us forward. His black car surged first. Mine came beside it.

15
00:05:12,007 --> 00:05:30,836
We reached the first joint together, wheels striking the metal seam
with a hard ringing sound. The platform dipped beneath us, rose again,
and the sea came up level with the lower edge of my window. Nigga,
that got my attention. Aurelio knew the course. He had practiced.

16
00:05:31,086 --> 00:05:54,463
His car moved with ugly confidence, taking the center of each platform
and forcing me toward the edge. I let him have the first bend. Stayed
close. Watched the way the floating road lifted before the next joint.
Behind us, Inez and Cassius ran side by side in red and white, their
cars bright against the blue water. Sabine held fifth.

17
00:05:54,713 --> 00:06:15,316
Adrian stayed back, patient after discovering twice that my existence
complicated his wagers. At the halfway point, the duke struck my rear
quarter. Lightly. Deliberately. The Lamborghini moved toward the edge.
I caught it. Nigga, I said, you want to make this personal in public?
He hit me again.

18
00:06:15,566 --> 00:06:35,633
This time I let the car move farther, gave him the space he expected
to take and watched him overcommit into it. His front wheels crossed
the seam at the wrong angle. The black car bounced, lost its clean
line and fell half a length behind. I took the center. The crowd on
the fort's quay began screaming.

19
00:06:35,883 --> 00:06:59,777
We hit the final ramp together, blue and black climbing out of the
sea. I landed first on the stone road, turned beneath the fort wall
and headed back toward the second crossing. The duke followed. Close.
He was furious now, but still good. That mattered. A bad driver could
have excused himself. Aurelio knew exactly how well I was beating him.

20
00:07:00,027 --> 00:07:16,347
On the return crossing, a platform ahead of us shifted sharply. One of
its holding boats had moved away. The floating road opened into a
narrow gap. Not huge. Huge would have made the trap obvious enough for
everybody to stop.

21
00:07:16,597 --> 00:07:35,204
This was the kind of gap meant to catch one car wrong and turn the
rest of the race into a tragic discussion about conditions. I saw the
duke look toward it. Then toward me. Nigga, there it was. I didn't
call the thunder. I called his bluff. I stayed beside him. Closer.

22
00:07:35,454 --> 00:07:56,924
So close he could not change his line without hitting me hard enough
for every camera to see the choice. The gap came toward us. His hands
tightened on the wheel. He braked. I didn't. The Lamborghini left the
edge, cleared the broken space and landed on the next platform with a
crack that went through my teeth. The car stayed straight.

23
00:07:57,174 --> 00:08:19,946
Behind me, Aurelio's black machine hit the gap slower, nose dipping,
rear wheels lifting before the front caught the far side. It landed
badly. Its front bumper tore away. I kept going. The other drivers
slowed and took the gap under the marshals' direction. The race wasn't
over. The trap hadn't swallowed anybody.

24
00:08:20,196 --> 00:08:45,516
The duke had simply damaged his own car trying to arrange the shape of
my fear. Nigga, expensive lesson. We came off the floating road onto
the island circuit. The coast flashed past my windows. Villages,
gardens, the opera house, the bakery where Julian's mother stood
waving a blue cloth as I passed. Inez caught the duke before the
palace hill. Cassius caught him after.

25
00:08:45,766 --> 00:09:09,922
Aurelio tried to hold them off. Couldn't. The black car's damaged
front scraped at every dip, throwing sparks beneath the gold paint. He
had enough speed to remain dangerous and not enough control to look
graceful doing it. I crossed the finish first. Inez second. Cassius
third. The duke came fourth, his car smoking, his face so still it
looked painted.

26
00:09:10,172 --> 00:09:31,156
Sabine climbed out behind him and walked straight to me. I can confirm
it, she said. What? You're impossible. Nigga, peer review. She laughed
and shook my hand with both of hers. The sixth key was brought forward
by Lord Orlan, who now looked like he had begun rehearsing a life in
which he worked for somebody else.

27
00:09:31,406 --> 00:09:51,691
He held the cushion out at arm's length. I took the key. Behind him,
the duke stepped out of his ruined car. The crowd booed. Not
everybody. Enough. He looked up at the palace, then back at me. You
haven't won the island. One more race. One more road. Nigga, I like
roads. He came closer.

28
00:09:51,941 --> 00:10:13,415
For the first time there were no cameras between us, only at our
sides. His voice dropped. I can make the island empty before you claim
it. My smile went away. He saw it and thought he had found something
useful. I can burn every car, sink every ship, silence every stage.
You can arrive first to ashes.

29
00:10:13,665 --> 00:10:35,719
I looked at him for a long moment. Then I took the key off its ribbon
and held it between two fingers. Nigga, you keep mistaking your last
chance to walk away for suspense. His eyes hardened. I didn't raise my
voice. You touch my people, your last race happens on foot. He stepped
back. His guards closed around him.

30
00:10:35,969 --> 00:10:54,596
The crowd parted as he walked toward the palace gates. At the top of
the steps stood the stranger in the dark uniform. This time the
stranger smiled at me openly. Like he had enjoyed every second. Shawna
came beside me. What'd he say? I told her. All of it.

31
00:10:54,846 --> 00:11:11,044
She looked toward the palace, then down at the six keys in the case
Porsche held open beside us. Then we don't leave everybody here alone
tonight. No. Nigga, good. We took over the opera house before sunset.
Not by force.

32
00:11:11,294 --> 00:11:33,801
Valentina opened the doors and told us which rooms had beds, which
corridors reached the street and which windows stuck when the sea wind
turned. The company stayed. Workers brought food. Rocco sent supplies.
Celadon kept the Sovereign Sun ready in the harbor, its lights bright,
its crew finally working for a captain they trusted.

33
00:11:34,051 --> 00:11:57,502
Tank and Bruno stood at the main doors while people came and went.
Mercedes and Porsche organized rooms with the efficiency of women who
had spent their adult lives judging whether a strange building wanted
anything good for them. Nero and Esteban sat with the old island
mechanics, drawing the final race course from memory. No secret
instructions.

34
00:11:57,752 --> 00:12:19,990
Just the shape of roads they loved and the places the duke had
changed. Shawna rehearsed with Rosa and the full company. I stood in
the back of the hall listening. Outside, the island waited for the
night. Inside, my wife filled the room with a voice the duke had
promised to silence. Nigga, he was going to have to come through me
first.
